\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison---008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON)---differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 0.97\times$ (eval 503.0). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 27 of 30 experimental configurations across two environments (AUROC${=}0.957$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
